ance.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    5435 Nov 11 09:43 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    4977 Nov 11 09:43 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   86458 Nov 11 09:43 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_094241.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   21616 Nov 11 09:42 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 09:43 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 09:43 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3342 Nov 11 09:45 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7671 Nov 11 10:04 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7671 Nov 11 10:03 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   24894 Nov 11 10:03 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   36481 Nov 11 10:04 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 09:42 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 10:02 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:51 scripts\ndrwxr-xr-x 6 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 10:01 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 09:47 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 09:47 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 09:42 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026)\n\n**Status:** ready  \n**Title (provisional):** Gated Weighted-Mondrian Split Conformal Prediction for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families\n\n## Central question\nOn synthetic softmax-output (simplex-valued) families, does a gated, shift-aware Mondrian split-conformal procedure give a better coverage/slice-reliability/set-size trade-off than standard LAC, APS, RAPS and class-wise split conformal under a single evaluator-owned multi-axis reliability protocol, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?\n\n## Central thesis\nUnder a fixed multi-axis protocol (marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, set-size ratio, negative controls), a Mondrian LAC procedure with a gated domain-classifier reweighting step attains a higher composite reliability score than the best of four standard conformal baselines on sealed final data (qualified; positive margin with a bootstrap interval excluding zero), while remaining exact-in-marginal-coverage on exchangeable cases and leaving non-trivial residual under-coverage on shifted cases and worst slices, so the result is conditional on the declared synthetic families and must not be extrapolated.\n\n## Narrative rationale\nThe only completed, qualified evidence is a sealed final evaluation (5 seeded runs, 0 failed episodes, all 3 negative controls passed) of the frozen search winner against evaluator-implemented baselines, plus a 3-variant, 3-seed development comparison. This supports a focused empirical paper that (i) states the evaluation protocol as the first-class object (what a reader should measure when judging conformal sets on compositional outputs, including one-sided under-coverage metrics, stratified slices, and negative controls), (ii) presents one method (Mondrian LAC + gated weighting) as the studied procedure, and (iii) reports headline margin with uncertainty and the residual failure modes honestly. Thesis-first, not chronology: the three search variants appear only as a nested design comparison. No real-data (e.g. CIFAR-10) results, no per-baseline breakdown and no per-case results exist in the artifacts, so claims stay inside the synthetic families; that scope limitation is itself part of the story. The final outcome is 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome), so no failed-qualification disclosure is needed, but the unverified diagnostics trust and the limits of the evidence must be stated.\n\n## Framing decision\nNot edit mode (no previous_paper / aggregated / extra_tasks). Prior story status: rejected (none exists). No downstream_study in paper_data; final_evaluation read independently: outcome `qualified`, status completed.\n\n## Method groups\n- **Standard split-conformal baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise)** (baseline): Final evaluator scores them on identical data with the same loss; only the best baseline score (final.metric.best_baseline_score) and the paired margin with bootstrap CI are retained. Individual baseline scores and identity of the best one are NOT in the artifacts and must be stated as unavailable. Trivial references (uncalibrated top-mass, full set, argmax only) exist in the evaluator but have no retained scores; mention at most as protocol components.\n- **Mondrian LAC split conformal with confidence-bin/class-conditional groups (development variant 1)** (ablation): Base procedure of the nested variant family; its search-stage metrics (combined score 0.868, shift gap 0.0613, worst-slice gap 0.0497, set ratio 0.166) give the reference point for what the weighting step changes.\n- **Gated cross-fitted domain-classifier weighted Mondrian conformal (frozen selected procedure)** (proposed): Search winner (tied top search score 0.9192 with variant 3) and the object of the sealed final evaluation; exact unweighted fallback when no shift is detected keeps exchangeable coverage and negative controls safe by design.\n- **Group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds on top of variant 2 (development variant 3)** (ablation): Produced search metrics and per-seed scores identical to variant 2 in all retained fields; report this as a null difference. Do not attribute the cause (e.g. gate fallback) unless the program log/source is read and cited; otherwise cause is unknown.\n\n## Results focus\n- Sealed final comparison: final score 0.8416 vs best baseline 0.8029, paired margin 0.0387 with bootstrap 95% interval [0.0274, 0.0499] (final.metric.score_margin_*); clarify the interval is a bootstrap over seeded runs within cases, aggregated equally across cases, and the baseline identity is not retained.\n- Five per-seed final scores (0.812 to 0.862) to show run-to-run spread; 0 failed episodes; controls passed fraction 1.0.\n- Reliability profile of the final procedure: exchangeable marginal under-coverage gap 0.0017, set-size ratio 0.209, worst-slice gap 0.0675, shift-case marginal gap 0.0536 (descriptive only), framed explicitly as residual limitations.\n- Development-stage nested comparison (variant 1 -> 2 -> 3; 3 of 3 attempts scored, none unscored): shift gap 0.0613 -> 0.0201, worst-slice gap 0.0497 -> 0.0376, set ratio 0.166 -> 0.181, exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; variant 3 identical to 2. Label as search-stage, 3 seeds, different cases than final; never merge with final numbers.\n- Note the search-to-final score drop (0.919 -> 0.842) without attributing it to overfitting or a specific cause: search and final use different seeds/case sets and the artifacts do not isolate the cause.\n- Theory as background, checked only where the artifacts allow: finite-sample split-conformal marginal guarantee (coverage >= 1-alpha, <= 1-alpha+1/(n+1)), Mondrian group-conditional guarantee, and weighted conformal under known likelihood ratio (Tibshirani et al.). Compare to observed exchangeable gap 0.0017 (one-sided). Do not present any new theorem unless the writer derives it in the text; the grounded artifacts contain no theory-vs-experiment numbers beyond that gap.\n\n## Figure plan\n- Fig 1 (page 1-2, concept overview): the protocol at a glance - simplex-valued softmax output -> calibration/test split -> conformal procedure under study (Mondrian LAC + gated weighting) vs evaluator baselines -> three score axes (marginal under-coverage 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) + three negative controls.\n- Fig 2: sealed final comparison - panel (a) final score vs best-baseline score with paired margin and 95% bootstrap interval; panel (b) five per-seed final scores.\n- Fig 3: reliability profile / development-stage nested variants - small-multiple dot panels for shift gap, worst-slice gap and set-size ratio across the three variants (search stage), with per-seed scores panel.\n\n## Figure specs\n- `protocol_overview` (diagram, generate-concept-figure, full_width, width 1.0, <= 1 panels): Glance-value overview of the evaluated object: simplex outputs, conformal procedure, evaluator-owned baselines, three-part loss, negative controls, and the scope boundary (synthetic families only). Layout: ICLR template is single-column (text width ~5.5 in); a left-to-right pipeline with ~6 labelled boxes needs the full text width for 8pt+ labels. Place on page 1 or 2.\n- `final_comparison` (dot_plot_with_interval, matplotlib, full_width, width 0.9, <= 2 panels): Sealed-final headline: procedure score vs best baseline score with paired margin CI, plus per-seed spread. Layout: Two compact panels side by side (score+margin interval; five per-seed dots) fit at ~0.9 of text width with readable ticks; shrinking below this would crush the interval annotation. Export PDF + 300 DPI PNG.\n- `variant_profile` (small_multiples_dot_plot, matplotlib, full_width, width 1.0, <= 4 panels): Show what the weighting step changes in the development-stage comparison (shift gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio, per-seed score) and that variant 3 is identical to variant 2; clearly marked as search-stage. Layout: Four panels in a 2x2 grid (or 1x4 only if labels stay >=8pt), three categories each; the full text width keeps axis labels and variant names legible. No progression axis; variants are categorical, not chronological.\n\n## Supplementary plan\nProvide reproducibility detail, full metric tables and implementation specifics that support but are not essential to the central thesis.\n- **Full metric tables for final and development evaluations**: Table of all retained final metrics (final score, best baseline score, margin and CI, exchangeable marginal gap, set-size ratio, worst-slice gap, shift gap, controls fraction, failed episodes) and per-seed final scores and durations; table of search-stage metrics and per-seed scores for the three variants. (sources: final.metric.final_score, final.metric.best_baseline_score, final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline, final.metric.score_margin_ci_lower, final.metric.score_margin_ci_upper, final.metric.mean_marginal_coverage_gap_exchangeable, final.metric.mean_set_size_ratio, final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap, final.metric.shift_coverage_gap, final.metric.controls_passed_fraction, final.metric.n_failed_episodes, final.evidence.episodes, result.top_program_01, result.top_program_02, result.top_program_03)\n- **Procedure specification of the studied conformal method**: Pseudo-code and hyperparameters of Mondrian LAC grouping (class-conditional for K<=10, confidence bins for K>10), cross-fitted ridge-logistic domain classifier on summary features of the softmax vector, positive-part shrinkage max(0, 1 - d/LR), clipped weights, per-test-row weighted thresholds with test mass at +inf, effective-sample-size margin and exact unweighted fallback; wire protocol limits (chunking, call budget). Taken from the staged program source and idea bundle. (sources: result.best_correct_score, result.top_program_01)\n- **Evaluation protocol details and scope**: Definition of the per-episode loss (0.5*clipped marginal under-coverage/alpha + 0.25*worst-slice under-coverage + 0.25*set-size ratio), slice definitions (class slices for K<=10; set-size and max-probability strata for K>10, minimum slice count 30), negative controls C1-C3, declared families and K range, equal-weight case aggregation, bootstrap margin interval, correctness conditions; explicit non-claims (real data, K>100, n_cal<200, simplex regression targets). (sources: final.qualification.outcome, final.qualification.status, final.qualification.correct, final.evidence.episodes)\n- **Theory background and limitation notes**: Statements and proof sketches (or citations) for the finite-sample marginal bound, Mondrian conditional guarantee and weighted-conformal guarantee, with a note on where each assumption is violated by shift families; discussion that one-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage. (sources: final.metric.mean_marginal_coverage_gap_exchangeable, final.metric.shift_coverage_gap)\n- **LLM Usage and reproducibility statement**: Required ICLR LLM-usage disclosure (the studied procedure was proposed and implemented by an LLM-driven search agent over three attempts; paper written with LLM assistance), seeds (search 101-103, final 9001-9005), per-run durations (about 4-8 s per final seeded run). (sources: final.evidence.episodes, result.total_attempts)\n\n## Forbidden headings\nSearch Trajectory, Iteration History, Milestones, Progression of Attempts, Experiment A / Experiment B, Discovery Process, What We Tried First, Campaign Summary\n\n## Forbidden phrases\ncorrectness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker_01, first valid result, the agent discovered, in cycle 1, state of the art, we prove (unless a derivation is included), generalizes to real data, outperforms all conformal methods, improvement over the incumbent\n\n## Notes for writer\n- Use reader-facing terms: model variant, configuration, seeded run. Call the metric 'composite reliability score' (1 minus the evaluator loss). Say 'search-stage score' for combined_score and 'sealed final score' for final_score; never reuse one label for different thresholds. No thresholded rate is needed.\n- Final outcome is 'qualified' (final.qualification.*), status completed, correct true. Do not imply failure; but state that candidate diagnostics trust is 'unverified' and that all numbers are aggregates retained by the evaluator.\n- Unknowns to state as unknown, not infer: identity and individual scores of baselines, per-case/per-family results, alpha and sample sizes of final cases (sealed), cause of search-to-final score drop, why variant 3 equals variant 2, which shift family drives the shift gap. Do not claim a statistical-vs-validity cause for any residual gap.\n- The marginal and slice gaps are one-sided under-coverage shortfalls (max(0, 1-alpha-coverage)); over-coverage is penalised only via set size. Say so, since a small gap does not show coverage is tight. The exchangeable gap 0.0017 is compatible with the finite-sample guarantee but is not a test of it.\n- Shift-case coverage is descriptive per the task contract; the shift gap 0.0536 (and worst-slice gap 0.0675) are residual failures, not successes. Report them alongside the headline margin. Positive margin means higher composite score under this specific loss weighting (0.5/0.25/0.25), which is evaluator-defined; do not present it as dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained.\n- Interpret the margin CI carefully: it is a bootstrap over seeded runs within each case, equal-weighted over cases; it does not capture variation across case designs or data-generating families beyond those evaluated.\n- Search variants are nested development steps (variant 2 adds gated weighting to 1; variant 3 adds QR thresholds to 2), 3 seeds each, on different cases than the final; present as a design comparison, not an ablation with significance. All 3 attempts were scored; no unscored attempts.\n- Related work: ground in references.bib entries actually retrieved (e.g. Ding et al. class-conditional CP, Gibbs et al. conditional guarantees, Melki et al. group-conditional QR calibration, Kandinsky CP, Dabah temperature scaling and CP); cite starter-literature items (Vovk 2005, Tibshirani 2019 covariate shift, Angelopoulos 2020/2021, Aitchison 1982) only if present in references.bib. Do not cite anything not in references.bib. Frame novelty modestly: composition of known pieces (Mondrian, weighted conformal, gating) plus protocol emphasis; do not claim a new compositional-geometry method, since the procedure works on summary features of probabilities, not on Aitchison-geometry transforms; mention Aitchison geometry only as motivation/limitation.\n- ICLR 2026 brief: 8-9 body pages, double-blind, single-column, LLM Usage section (appendix allowed), no author identifiers or repository links. Fill the budget with: intro + contributions (~1), related work (~0.75), problem setup/protocol and theory background (~1.5), method (~1.25), experimental setup (~0.75), results with Figs 2-3 and a compact table (~1.75), residual-failure/limitation analysis (~0.75), discussion/conclusion (~0.5), plus Fig 1 on page 1-2. No padding; if evidence is thin, shorten rather than pad but meet the 75%-full rule for the last page.\n- Figures: export PDF plus 300 DPI PNG from source; reference the PDF in LaTeX; no raster upscaling (raster fallback >= 2400 px for full width). Panels <= 4, categories <= 6 per panel, series <= 4. The only generate-concept-figure use is protocol_overview; other figures use matplotlib. Fig 1 near page 1-2.\n- Do not state any CIFAR-10 or real-data result; none exist. The task's stated real-data options are unexercised and should be listed as a limitation/future work.\nfigures:\nfinal_comparison.pdf\nfinal_comparison.pdf.prov.json\nfinal_comparison.png\nfinal_comparison.png.prov.json\nloss_decomposition.pdf\nloss_decomposition.pdf.prov.json\nloss_decomposition.png\nloss_decomposition.png.prov.json\nposthoc\nprotocol_overview.png\nprotocol_overview.prompts.json\nvariant_profile.pdf\nvariant_profile.pdf.prov.json\nvariant_profile.png\nvariant_profile.png.prov.json\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json\nls: cannot access 'validation_feedback*': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'paper_review_feedback*': No such file or directory\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_094241.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_094241.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
