{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DHzCtzNmEcZEuWjGAqC9EK","type":"tool_result","content":"29:\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n36:Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n39:\\section{Introduction}\n43:\\emph{does a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step achieve a better composite reliability score than standard split-conformal baselines, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?}\n49:(ii) We specify one concrete procedure, composed from known pieces (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, a held-out gate), and state which of its properties are exact and which are only approximate when the weights are estimated.\n56:\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n60:\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n62:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n71:Our contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n73:\\section{Method}\n74:\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\label{sec:setup}\n78:\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\n87:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n88:The evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n93:\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n94:\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\n95:With exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n97:\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\n100:For $G=y$ the cell is observable only at calibration time, and the set $\\{k:1-p_k(x)\\le q_k\\}$ covers the true label $y$ with class-conditional probability at least $1-\\alpha$ because the event is $\\{s(x,y)\\le q_y\\}$. For $G$ a function of $x$ alone the cell is known at prediction time. The guarantee is conditional on the cell and does not extend to other partitions: when $K>10$ the evaluator's slices (strata of set size and of maximum probability in quartiles) differ from the Mondrian confidence bins used here. The implemented thresholds also depart from the proposition: lowering $\\alpha_g$ by a margin can only raise $q_g$, but shrinking $q_g$ toward the pooled threshold can lower it, so the implemented procedure has no exact cell-conditional guarantee, only the exact quantile at level $\\alpha_g$ does. The data-dependent gate below is \\emph{not} covered by this argument: it uses unlabelled calibration and test features, it treats the two groups asymmetrically, and we have not derived a guarantee for the draws on which it activates under exchangeability. We therefore use the proposition only as background for the fallback branch.\n102:\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\n109:\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\n110:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n112:\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n113:\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\n114:The score of label $k$ is $1-p_k(x)$ (LAC). For $K\\le10$ the Mondrian groups are the labels, because the evaluator's slices are classes; for $K>10$ they are confidence bins of the maximum probability (at most 10 equal-mass bins, at least 80 calibration rows each), a function of $x$ alone. Each group's threshold is the finite-sample conformal quantile at level $\\alpha_g=\\alpha-z\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/n_g+k_g/M)}$ with $z=0.6$ per group and $0.7$ for the pooled threshold, where $M$ is the number of test rows and $k_g$ the number of groups; group thresholds are shrunk toward the pooled threshold with weight $n_g/(n_g+20)$, and groups too small for a finite quantile use the pooled value. The set is $\\{k: 1-p_k(x)\\le q_{g}\\}$.\n118:Each probability vector is mapped to five summary features (logit of the top probability, top-two log ratio, normalised entropy, mean top-five log probability minus the top log probability, log normalised Gini impurity), standardised with calibration moments and clipped to $\\pm8$. As test chunks arrive, a 2-fold cross-fitted ridge-penalised logistic classifier (penalty 1, class-balanced weights) separates calibration from all test rows seen so far, and the held-out likelihood-ratio statistic $\\mathrm{LR}=2(\\ell_{\\mathrm{full}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{null}})$ against an intercept-only model gives a positive-part shrinkage factor $\\beta=\\max(0,1-d/\\mathrm{LR})$ with $d=5$. If fewer than 40 test rows have been seen, or $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$, or any quantity is non-finite, the unweighted Mondrian thresholds are used verbatim. Otherwise weights $w=\\exp(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta z,\\pm\\log 8))$ are built from the centred held-out log-odds $z$, and each test row receives weighted per-group thresholds with its own weight at $+\\infty$ and effective-sample-size margins ($n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w)^2/\\sum w^2$). Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is typically non-positive; it is not guaranteed to be inactive on every draw, and its activation frequency was not logged.\n121:\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n122:\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n125:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n130:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n131:\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\n167:\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\n180:\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\n181:Figure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\n189:\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n193:\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\n196:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n206:(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n227:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to select the configuration, so development numbers are optimistic selection-biased proxies; the sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n231:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. A stronger study built on the same protocol would additionally report per-baseline and per-case scores, a within-case ablation that separates the Mondrian grouping, the gate and the weighting, activation logs for the gate, and at least one evaluation on classifier outputs from a natural dataset. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n233:\\section{Conclusion}\n234:We studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n244:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n246:\\subsection{Procedure specification}\n259:Mondrian & class-conditional if & $K\\le10$ \\\\\n260:Mondrian & confidence bins ($K>10$): max bins / min rows per bin & 10 / 80 \\\\\n276:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n280:\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n289:V1 Mondrian LAC & 0.8683 & 0.00028 & 0.0497 & 0.0613 & 0.166 \\\\\n333:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n341:\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\n342:The figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~\\ref{sec:related} rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only; several standard references on split-conformal theory, covariate-shift weighting and compositional data analysis are not in the retrieved bibliography and are therefore stated as background without citation.\n344:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bfae6f9-e277-47df-a508-24d3af227d39","uuid":"4f9bc969-c5e1-4c1b-b05d-4716a9cb5b4e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:06:15.566Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"29:\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n36:Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n39:\\section{Introduction}\n43:\\emph{does a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step achieve a better composite reliability score than standard split-conformal baselines, and where do its guarantees visibly stop holding?}\n49:(ii) We specify one concrete procedure, composed from known pieces (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, a held-out gate), and state which of its properties are exact and which are only approximate when the weights are estimated.\n56:\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n60:\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n62:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n71:Our contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n73:\\section{Method}\n74:\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\label{sec:setup}\n78:\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\n87:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n88:The evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n93:\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n94:\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\n95:With exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n97:\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\n100:For $G=y$ the cell is observable only at calibration time, and the set $\\{k:1-p_k(x)\\le q_k\\}$ covers the true label $y$ with class-conditional probability at least $1-\\alpha$ because the event is $\\{s(x,y)\\le q_y\\}$. For $G$ a function of $x$ alone the cell is known at prediction time. The guarantee is conditional on the cell and does not extend to other partitions: when $K>10$ the evaluator's slices (strata of set size and of maximum probability in quartiles) differ from the Mondrian confidence bins used here. The implemented thresholds also depart from the proposition: lowering $\\alpha_g$ by a margin can only raise $q_g$, but shrinking $q_g$ toward the pooled threshold can lower it, so the implemented procedure has no exact cell-conditional guarantee, only the exact quantile at level $\\alpha_g$ does. The data-dependent gate below is \\emph{not} covered by this argument: it uses unlabelled calibration and test features, it treats the two groups asymmetrically, and we have not derived a guarantee for the draws on which it activates under exchangeability. We therefore use the proposition only as background for the fallback branch.\n102:\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\n109:\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\n110:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n112:\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n113:\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\n114:The score of label $k$ is $1-p_k(x)$ (LAC). For $K\\le10$ the Mondrian groups are the labels, because the evaluator's slices are classes; for $K>10$ they are confidence bins of the maximum probability (at most 10 equal-mass bins, at least 80 calibration rows each), a function of $x$ alone. Each group's threshold is the finite-sample conformal quantile at level $\\alpha_g=\\alpha-z\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/n_g+k_g/M)}$ with $z=0.6$ per group and $0.7$ for the pooled threshold, where $M$ is the number of test rows and $k_g$ the number of groups; group thresholds are shrunk toward the pooled threshold with weight $n_g/(n_g+20)$, and groups too small for a finite quantile use the pooled value. The set is $\\{k: 1-p_k(x)\\le q_{g}\\}$.\n118:Each probability vector is mapped to five summary features (logit of the top probability, top-two log ratio, normalised entropy, mean top-five log probability minus the top log probability, log normalised Gini impurity), standardised with calibration moments and clipped to $\\pm8$. As test chunks arrive, a 2-fold cross-fitted ridge-penalised logistic classifier (penalty 1, class-balanced weights) separates calibration from all test rows seen so far, and the held-out likelihood-ratio statistic $\\mathrm{LR}=2(\\ell_{\\mathrm{full}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{null}})$ against an intercept-only model gives a positive-part shrinkage factor $\\beta=\\max(0,1-d/\\mathrm{LR})$ with $d=5$. If fewer than 40 test rows have been seen, or $\\beta\\le10^{-3}$, or any quantity is non-finite, the unweighted Mondrian thresholds are used verbatim. Otherwise weights $w=\\exp(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta z,\\pm\\log 8))$ are built from the centred held-out log-odds $z$, and each test row receives weighted per-group thresholds with its own weight at $+\\infty$ and effective-sample-size margins ($n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w)^2/\\sum w^2$). Under exchangeability the gate is intended to be inactive because the held-out likelihood ratio is typically non-positive; it is not guaranteed to be inactive on every draw, and its activation frequency was not logged.\n121:\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n122:\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n125:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n130:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n131:\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\n167:\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\n180:\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\n181:Figure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\n189:\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n193:\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\n196:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n206:(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n227:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to select the configuration, so development numbers are optimistic selection-biased proxies; the sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n231:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. A stronger study built on the same protocol would additionally report per-baseline and per-case scores, a within-case ablation that separates the Mondrian grouping, the gate and the weighting, activation logs for the gate, and at least one evaluation on classifier outputs from a natural dataset. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n233:\\section{Conclusion}\n234:We studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n244:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n246:\\subsection{Procedure specification}\n259:Mondrian & class-conditional if & $K\\le10$ \\\\\n260:Mondrian & confidence bins ($K>10$): max bins / min rows per bin & 10 / 80 \\\\\n276:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n280:\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n289:V1 Mondrian LAC & 0.8683 & 0.00028 & 0.0497 & 0.0613 & 0.166 \\\\\n333:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n341:\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\n342:The figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~\\ref{sec:related} rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only; several standard references on split-conformal theory, covariate-shift weighting and compositional data analysis are not in the retrieved bibliography and are therefore stated as background without citation.\n344:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
