{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QRkzij3V8FUWdDvye1V3P1","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\nExact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\nSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal methods in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n\n\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\nConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied and tutorial treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging, histopathology, pattern recognition and software-tutorial venues \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF,Rueda2007ProgressIP,Löfström2022TutorialFU}. We characterise these works only to the extent of their titles and venues, which is all the retrieval metadata record.\n\n\\paragraph{Positioning.}\nOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\nThe evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n\nThree negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py#control_verdicts]]\n\n\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\nWith exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n\n\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\nLet $G$ be a partition cell function of $(x,y)$ and let the calibration scores of cell $g$ be $s_1,\\dots,s_{n_g}$, exchangeable with the test score $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ conditional on the test point lying in cell $g$, with no ties. Take $q_g$ equal to the $k_g$-th smallest calibration score, $k_g=\\lceil(n_g+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$. Then $\\Pr(s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g\\mid G=g)\\ge1-\\alpha$.\n\\emph{Proof sketch.} By exchangeability the rank of $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ among the $n_g+1$ scores is uniform on $\\{1,\\dots,n_g+1\\}$. The event $s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g$ holds exactly when this rank is at most $k_g$, which has probability $k_g/(n_g+1)\\ge1-\\alpha$; ties only increase the probability. $\\square$\nFor $G=y$ the cell is observable only at calibration time, and the set $\\{k:1-p_k(x)\\le q_k\\}$ covers the true label $y$ with class-conditional probability at least $1-\\alpha$ because the event is $\\{s(x,y)\\le q_y\\}$. For $G$ a function of $x$ alone the cell is known at prediction time. The guarantee is conditional on the cell and does not extend to other partitions: when $K>10$ the evaluator's slices (strata of set size and of maximum probability in quartiles) differ from the Mondrian confidence bins used here. The implemented thresholds also depart from the proposition: lowering $\\alpha_g$ by a margin can only raise $q_g$, but shrinking $q_g$ toward the pooled threshold can lower it, so the implemented procedure has no exact cell-conditional guarantee, only the exact quantile at level $\\alpha_g$ does. The data-dependent gate below is \\emph{not} covered by this argument: it uses unlabelled calibration and test features, it treats the two groups asymmetrically, and we have not derived a guarantee for the draws on which it activates under exchangeability. We therefore use the proposition only as background for the fallback branch.\n\n\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\nIf test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n\n\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\nUnder the normal approximation to the Beta law, calibration-induced coverage is $C\\approx\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2\\approx\\alpha(1-\\alpha)/(n+2)$ and $\\mu\\ge1-\\alpha$. The expected one-sided shortfall is $\\sigma\\,f(\\delta)$ with $f(\\delta)=\\varphi(\\delta)-\\delta(1-\\Phi(\\delta))$ and $\\delta=(\\mu-(1-\\alpha))/\\sigma$. An exact-level quantile has $\\delta\\approx0$ and $f(0)=0.399$, whereas shifting the level by $0.7\\sigma$ gives $f(0.7)=0.143$, about 36\\% of the unmargined value, at the price of larger sets. This is the motivation for the variance-aware margins used below (multipliers 0.7 pooled, 0.6 per group). It is a heuristic derivation about calibration noise only; we have no unmargined ablation, so it is not checked experimentally here.\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bfae6f9-e277-47df-a508-24d3af227d39","uuid":"54190b2b-a18a-42de-895e-db5d3020ac2f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:06:17.985Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\nExact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\nSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Related uses of conformal methods in neural prediction include Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n\n\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\nConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied and tutorial treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging, histopathology, pattern recognition and software-tutorial venues \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF,Rueda2007ProgressIP,Löfström2022TutorialFU}. We characterise these works only to the extent of their titles and venues, which is all the retrieval metadata record.\n\n\\paragraph{Positioning.}\nOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\nThe evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss: LAC (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n\nThree negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py#control_verdicts]]\n\n\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\nWith exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements and we do not claim them as new.\n\n\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\nLet $G$ be a partition cell function of $(x,y)$ and let the calibration scores of cell $g$ be $s_1,\\dots,s_{n_g}$, exchangeable with the test score $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ conditional on the test point lying in cell $g$, with no ties. Take $q_g$ equal to the $k_g$-th smallest calibration score, $k_g=\\lceil(n_g+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$. Then $\\Pr(s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g\\mid G=g)\\ge1-\\alpha$.\n\\emph{Proof sketch.} By exchangeability the rank of $s_{\\mathrm{new}}$ among the $n_g+1$ scores is uniform on $\\{1,\\dots,n_g+1\\}$. The event $s_{\\mathrm{new}}\\le q_g$ holds exactly when this rank is at most $k_g$, which has probability $k_g/(n_g+1)\\ge1-\\alpha$; ties only increase the probability. $\\square$\nFor $G=y$ the cell is observable only at calibration time, and the set $\\{k:1-p_k(x)\\le q_k\\}$ covers the true label $y$ with class-conditional probability at least $1-\\alpha$ because the event is $\\{s(x,y)\\le q_y\\}$. For $G$ a function of $x$ alone the cell is known at prediction time. The guarantee is conditional on the cell and does not extend to other partitions: when $K>10$ the evaluator's slices (strata of set size and of maximum probability in quartiles) differ from the Mondrian confidence bins used here. The implemented thresholds also depart from the proposition: lowering $\\alpha_g$ by a margin can only raise $q_g$, but shrinking $q_g$ toward the pooled threshold can lower it, so the implemented procedure has no exact cell-conditional guarantee, only the exact quantile at level $\\alpha_g$ does. The data-dependent gate below is \\emph{not} covered by this argument: it uses unlabelled calibration and test features, it treats the two groups asymmetrically, and we have not derived a guarantee for the draws on which it activates under exchangeability. We therefore use the proposition only as background for the fallback branch.\n\n\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\nIf test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n\n\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\nUnder the normal approximation to the Beta law, calibration-induced coverage is $C\\approx\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2\\approx\\alpha(1-\\alpha)/(n+2)$ and $\\mu\\ge1-\\alpha$. The expected one-sided shortfall is $\\sigma\\,f(\\delta)$ with $f(\\delta)=\\varphi(\\delta)-\\delta(1-\\Phi(\\delta))$ and $\\delta=(\\mu-(1-\\alpha))/\\sigma$. An exact-level quantile has $\\delta\\approx0$ and $f(0)=0.399$, whereas shifting the level by $0.7\\sigma$ gives $f(0.7)=0.143$, about 36\\% of the unmargined value, at the price of larger sets. This is the motivation for the variance-aware margins used below (multipliers 0.7 pooled, 0.6 per group). It is a heuristic derivation about calibration noise only; we have no unmargined ablation, so it is not checked experimentally here.\n% @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#margin_constants]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
