{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019u4d5iDDyewTHyXrFNNt4E","type":"tool_result","content":"\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or o\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, s\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evidence inventory for the sealed final evaluation.}\n\\label{tab:inventory}\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.46\\textwidth}p{0.42\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nAvailable & Not retained (unknown) \\\\\n\\midrule\nAggregate score, best-baseline aggregate score, paired margin and bootstrap interval & Identity and individual scores of the four baselines \\\\\nFive per-seed scores; failed-episode count; control pass fraction & Per-case and per-family results; which cases drive shift and slice gaps \\\\\nExchangeable, shift, worst-slice and size-ratio aggregates & $\\alpha$, $K$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases \\\\\nSource of the studied procedure and of the evaluator code & Gate-activation frequency; cause of the development-to-final drop; cause of the V3--V2 tie \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\nThe synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage \n\n\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by re","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bfae6f9-e277-47df-a508-24d3af227d39","uuid":"b55e66a2-28e2-4d46-9b6a-96580b54ad3b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:08:09.710Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or o\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, s\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evidence inventory for the sealed final evaluation.}\n\\label{tab:inventory}\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.46\\textwidth}p{0.42\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nAvailable & Not retained (unknown) \\\\\n\\midrule\nAggregate score, best-baseline aggregate score, paired margin and bootstrap interval & Identity and individual scores of the four baselines \\\\\nFive per-seed scores; failed-episode count; control pass fraction & Per-case and per-family results; which cases drive shift and slice gaps \\\\\nExchangeable, shift, worst-slice and size-ratio aggregates & $\\alpha$, $K$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases \\\\\nSource of the studied procedure and of the evaluator code & Gate-activation frequency; cause of the development-to-final drop; cause of the V3--V2 tie \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\nThe synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage \n\n\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by re","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
